<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herero people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ovaherero</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Herero are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-herding people of central and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>. In the 19th century they contested land and herds with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oorlam">Oorlam</a> and Nama, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharero">Maharero</a> won their independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonker-afrikaner">Jonker Afrikaner</a>'s successors in the 1860s. Their rising against German rule in 1904 was crushed by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothar-von-trotha">Lothar von Trotha</a> in a campaign widely recognised as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herero-people.jpg" alt="Herero people" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bries · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herero_women.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Herero, or Ovaherero, speak Otjiherero, a Bantu language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>–Congo family. Unlike most Bantu, who are primarily subsistence farmers, they are traditionally pastoralists. In the 15th century the Herero migrated to what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> from the east and established themselves as herdsmen.</p><p>Early in the 19th century the Nama from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, who already had some firearms, entered the land, followed by white merchants and German missionaries. The Nama at first displaced the Herero, and bitter warfare between the two groups lasted for much of the century, before a period of cultural exchange. The death of Tjamuaha in 1861 led to hostilities with the Nama and made unity necessary, and on 15 June 1863 at Otjizingue the clans elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharero">Maharero</a> commander-in-chief. He later became the first Paramount Chief.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1883 the merchant Franz Adolf Eduard Lüderitz made a contract with local elders that later became the basis of German colonial rule, and the territory became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a>. Disputes over land and water arose, as did legal discrimination against the native population. Between 1893 and 1903 Herero and Nama land and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> were progressively taken by settlers, and in 1903 the Herero learned that they were to be placed in reservations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-maharero">Samuel Maharero</a>, who had become Paramount Chief in 1892 with the aid of the German governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-leutwein">Theodor Leutwein</a>, led a rising on 12 January 1904, which at first had success.</p><p>General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothar-von-trotha">Lothar von Trotha</a> took command in May 1904. In August the Herero were surrounded, with one route of escape into the desert, and were prevented from reaching waterholes; starvation followed. Von Trotha called the conflict a race war and issued an order that every Herero within the German borders would be shot. Roughly 80,000 Herero lived in the colony at the start of German rule; after the revolt about 15,000 remained, and about 65,000 had been killed. Survivors were held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> until 1915, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-maharero">Samuel Maharero</a> fled to Bechuanaland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a> are the mainstay of the Herero economy and a symbol of wealth. Men herd and trade cattle, while women milk them and prepare sour milk, called omaere. The Herero have a double descent system: a person traces heritage through the mother's lineage, the eanda, while clan leadership passes through the father's lineage, the oruzo. Most Herero today are Christians.</p><p>Women wear the long, voluminous dresses, called ohorokova, adopted from German missionaries in the late 19th century, with the horned headdress, the otjikaiva, which honours <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. On ceremonial occasions men wear military-style dress, including peaked caps and epaulettes, to honour fallen ancestors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herero people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Herero</b> rising of 1904 and the Nama war under Hendrik Witbooi were crushed by General Lothar von Trotha; tens of thousands died in the desert and in concentration camps such as Shark Island.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Herero</b>, Bantu-speaking cattle herders of central Namibia, had been raided for decades by Jonker Afrikaner's Orlam Afrikaner State.</blockquote><a href="/state/herero-paramountcy">Herero Paramountcy</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1823 his son Jonker Afrikaner moved north at the invitation of the Nama to help them against the <b>Herero</b>, and by about 1840 he had settled at Windhoek, which he made his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/orlam-afrikaner-state">Orlam Afrikaner State</a></li><li><blockquote>Jordan was murdered in 1886, and the settlers, under pressure from the <b>Herero</b> and with no outside support, renamed the state Lijdensrust and dissolved it in 1887.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-upingtonia">Republic of Upingtonia</a></li><li><blockquote>Hendrik Witbooi, grandson of Kido, saw himself as a Christian leader with a divine mission and fought long campaigns against the <b>Herero</b> for cattle and land.</blockquote><a href="/state/witbooi-nama">Witbooi Nama</a></li></ul></section>
